{"answer":"The claimed conclusion that √2 is rational is false. The displayed argument is valid only as a proof by contradiction: assuming √2=a/b in lowest terms, the equation a²=2b² does imply that both a and b are even, contradicting “lowest terms.” Thus no such rational representation exists, so √2 is irrational. The inference to evenness needs justification; it cannot merely be asserted.","approaches":[{"description":"Assume a lowest-terms rational representation, derive that each numerator and denominator is divisible by 2, and obtain a contradiction.","id":"parity-contradiction","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The parity argument below supplies the omitted justification for the evenness inference."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary contradiction assumption that √2 has a lowest-terms integer representation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"claim-rational-assumption","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-square","step-a-even","step-substitute","step-b-even","step-common-factor","step-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume that there are integers a,b with b ≠ 0, gcd(a,b)=1, and √2=a/b.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring the assumed equality.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-square-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a²=2b².","step_ids":["step-square"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived using the odd-square parity calculation in step-a-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-a-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, a is even.","step_ids":["step-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by substitution into a²=2b².","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-a-even","claim-square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-b-square-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b² is even.","step_ids":["step-substitute"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived using the odd-square parity calculation in step-b-even.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-b-square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"claim-b-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Under the temporary assumption, b is even.","step_ids":["step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The temporary assumption is discharged by the contradiction in step-contradiction.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["claim-rational-assumption","claim-a-even","claim-b-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"claim-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There is no lowest-terms integer representation √2=a/b; hence √2 is irrational.","step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-square","justification":"From √2=a/b, square both sides and multiply by b²; b ≠ 0.","statement":"a²=2b²."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"Step-square makes a² even. If a were odd, a=2q+1 for an integer q, so a²=4q(q+1)+1, which is odd. Therefore a is even.","statement":"a is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square","step-a-even"],"id":"step-substitute","justification":"Write a=2k because a is even. Then 4k²=2b², so b²=2k².","statement":"b² is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-substitute"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"If b were odd, the same calculation as in step-a-even would make b² odd, contrary to step-substitute.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-common-factor","justification":"Both a and b are divisible by 2.","statement":"gcd(a,b) is at least 2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-common-factor"],"id":"step-contradiction","justification":"This contradicts the temporary assumption gcd(a,b)=1, so that assumption is false.","statement":"No lowest-terms rational representation of √2 exists."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}

